ns with a fairly  simple and cheap treatment exercise  several studies have shown that regular  exercise can lead to a three-fold  increase in BDNF levels even a single  session can make a difference  exercise is effective at ramping up  neurogenesis and keeping those cells  integrated in the brain and while  exercise can increase adult neurogenesis  stress can impair it in animal models of  depression neurogenesis decreases under  experimental conditions where the  animals are placed under stress in these  same experiments administering and  depressant medications to the stressed  animals has been shown to restore  neurogenesis to healthy rates there are  other examples of people who  successfully increase the volume of  specific parts of their brains by  training or learning a new set of skills  not necessarily by growing new cells but  by increasing the connections between  them a Swedish neuroimaging study for  example tracked the changes in brain  volumes in a group of army recruits  learning Arabic Russian or dari  they compared these changes with those  in medical students who were also  studying intensively though the content  of their learning was very different by  the end of the study the army recruits  showed larger volumes and brain regions  that are involved in processing language  as well as our friend the hippocampus  which is necessary for new long-term  memories for facts and events compared  with the medical students who didn't  show measurable changes in brain size  and these change is correlated with how  well the recruits were doing those who  are mastering the new language and who  were trying harder showed greater  increases in the hippocampus and  language areas compared with recruits  who were struggling or not putting in as  much effort these results are similar to  a now same  study of hippocampus volumes and  students training to be black cab taxi  drivers in London the iconic black cabs  in London are known for their impressive  knowledge of all the streets and  landmarks within a six-mile radius of  Charing Cross because they're so  knowledgeable they charge a higher fee  and that makes their cabs the most  expensive in the city to get a license  to drive a black cab driver tasks to  pass a test called the knowledge it  takes most students two years of daily  work to pass it every day they spend  time in a classroom and then they get on  scooters and race around the city  learning and memorizing the best routes  to different places one seminal early  study compared the volume of the  hippocampus and black cab drivers with  the rest of the population and found a  difference their hippocampal volumes  were indeed different but this study  couldn't address the question of whether  the training itself led to the increase  or if people with larger volumes were  drawn to the job and were more likely to  pass so in a follow-up study the  neuroscientists tracks brain volumes in  a group of student students studying to  pass the knowledge and sure enough  everyone started off with hippocampal  volumes of similar size and when the  study's authors compared the volumes of  the students who passed the test with  those of students who failed they again  found differences the training itself if  it was successful was responsible for  hippocampal growth even juggling every  day for three months has been shown to  increase the volume of certain parts of  the brain including the hippocampus in  20-somethings and in sixty somethings  and while the 60 year olds weren't  juggling as well as the younger  counterparts with the same amount of  training they still showed increases in  brain volume but once the participants  in those experiments stopped juggling  their regions returned to their previous  sizes just like the rats who without an  effortful task to do lost a lot of the  new neurons that they had grown  talk about neuroplasticity however  without mentioning music that's because  musical training has long been used to  demonstrate plasticity in human brains  there are studies that show that  pianists have more of their cortex  devoted to processing sensory motor  information from the hand than the rest  of us and that musicians in general show  measurable brain differences in many  regions but is this nature or nurture at  least one compelling study has shown  that its musical training and not just  genes that's responsible for many of  these differences taking a group of six  year old public school children in  Boston who had no formal music training  scientists randomly assign them to two  groups one group received individual  piano lessons every week and the other  got to sing and play drums and vowels in  a music class at school the groups were  then matched for socioeconomic status  fifteen months later the scientists  scanned their brains again and found  that the group with individual music  lessons showed larger brain volumes in  three areas regions controlling motor  behavior hearing and the corpus callosum  the fiber track that joins the left and  right hemispheres of the brain so it  does seem that musical training can  drive brain volume increases in parts of  the brain that are engaged by the  activity as we get older we tend to  spend less time learning new things or  engaging our brains and effortful ways  and given that this effort is what seems  to benefit our cognitive function and  our brain structure in measurable ways  our lifestyle choices play a significant  role in whether or not we experience  cognitive decline with aging and that  experience in turn can affect our very  attitudes towards longevity for example  if you could choose how long would you  want to live I've been asked that  question twice now in a room full of  people and both times I was surprised at  what the majority of people said they  would choose unlike me it seems that  most people don't want to live longer  than 100  20 years the science writer David Ewing  Duncan conducted an online survey on  this question and found that out of more  than 30,000 responses more than half of  respondents agreed that somewhere around  80 is a good enough lifespan I'm in the  top or bottom depending on your point of  view of the 5% of people who really want  to live forever but perhaps I shouldn't  be so surprised given the hardships but  currently a company aging if continuing  to live just means continuing to suffer  and decline than sure at some point  enough is enough and the stigma that's  associated with old age underscores the  popular belief that things only get  worse  is that true at least in Britain and  Germany surveys of life satisfaction  have shown that it actually steadily  increases between age 40 and 70 if  you're Australian then you're likely to  find peak life satisfaction around age  80  so maybe many of those Duncan survey  respondents were on the right track but  if we managed to stave off physical and  mental decline and save enough money to  economically sustain an extended  retirement I suspect that most people  would push out their desired lifespan  perhaps even past 150 so how much do we  know about the basic biology of Aging  unfortunately at least in the u.s. the  amount of funding for the scientific  study of aging biology pales in  comparison with the amount devoted to  studying diseases of Aging that's a bit  backward in my opinion once we figured  out that smoking increases the risk of  lung cancer we instructed people to smut  stop smoking now we can't just tell  people top aging but shouldn't we be  racing to understand how aging affects  our bodies so that we can slow or  reverse the process rather than focusing  on  asked the vast majority of our resources  on the consequences when it comes to  cognition there are already hints that  interventions designed to keep the body  healthy like exercise have powerful  effects in terms of staving off  age-related mental decline but what  about those people who seem to be able  to stay mentally sharp well into old age  is there something we can learn from  these super Agers there are several labs  now studying not only healthy aging but  super healthy aging people who thrive an  old age and who score as well as people  who are 20 to 30 years younger in one  study for example super Agers are  defined as individuals over the age of  80 who score as well or better than  people who are 50 to 60 years old on a  set of cognitive tests in one study  involving around 30 of these people  scientists found that their anterior  cingulate cortex a region of the brain  responsible for cognitive control  resolving conflict keeping up motivation  and perseverance among other executive  functions was not only larger than that  of their 80 year old peers but also  bigger than the average size in  middle-aged people we still have a long  way to go before we can make any solid  conclusions about what factors combine  to support such successful aging  certainly genes are a factor for most of  us lifestyle choices have a greater  influence on our risk of death before  age 80 and genetics seem to account for  more of the individual variability in  overall health our overall health once  we reach our ninth decade of life in one  study super Agers are pretty varied  attorneys neuroscientists even one  individual who reportedly smokes a pack  of cigarettes a day and caps off each  evening with a martini but what they may  have in common reminds me a little of  our famous sister Mary from an earlier  lecture at least anecdotally they have  remained active members of their  community socializing  regularly and sure enough there's one  more brain finding in this group that's  been reported they have three to five  times more von Economo neurons in their  anterior cingulate cortex than their  peers these large cells are thought to  play a role in facilitating social  interaction and they are the topic of  our next lecture


